539d4b130713a","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts the singular values of a real matrix $A$ with its eigenvalues?","options":["Singular values, like eigenvalues, need $A$ to be square","Singular values, like eigenvalues, may be complex numbers","Singular values exist for any $m\\times n$ $A$ and are real and nonnegative","Singular values and eigenvalues agree up to sign for square $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cc599564a33b402f7896c864","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ be a real $m\\times n$ matrix. Which quantity equals the number of positive singular values of $A$?","options":["The number of columns $n$","The number of nonzero eigenvalues of $A$ (for square $A$)","The smaller dimension $\\min(m,n)$","The rank of $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fcc57a643decc0d7393e1afe","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $A$ be a real $m\\times n$ matrix with largest singular value $\\sigma_1$. What does $\\sigma_1$ measure?","options":["The largest absolute value of an eigenvalue of $A$","The largest length $\\|A\\boldsymbol{x}\\|$ over unit vectors $\\boldsymbol{x}$","The largest absolute value of an entry of $A$","The factor $|\\det A|$ by which $A$ scales volume"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"23402cbce76533b982c4b931","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What are the singular values $\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2$ of $A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&0\\\\0&-4\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$\\sigma_1=3$, $\\sigma_2=-4$","$\\sigma_1=16$, $\\sigma_2=9$","$\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=3$","$\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2=\\tfrac{7}{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bb6113ab8d63f53a4c121040","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What are the singular values $\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2$ of $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$, whose eigenvalues are both $0$?","options":["$\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2=0$","$\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=0$","$\\sigma_1=2$, $\\sigma_2=0$","$\\sigma_1=\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ba16251f96f0998b1742a986","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1&0\\\\0&0&2\\end{pmatrix}$, a $2\\times3$ matrix. Using the smaller product $AA^{\\top}$, what are its singular values $\\sigma_1\\ge\\sigma_2$?","options":["$\\sigma_1=4$, $\\sigma_2=2$","$\\sigma_1=\\sqrt{6}$, $\\sigma_2=0$","$\\sigma_1=2$, $\\sigma_2=\\sqrt{2}$","$\\sigma_1=2$, $\\sigma_2=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b316c78d3504607c9a351bb6","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $Q$ be a $2\\times2$ orthogonal matrix. What are the singular values of $-3Q$?","options":["$3$ and $3$","$-3$ and $-3$","$9$ and $9$","$3$ and $\\tfrac{1}{3}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a780608a108010d4d2440f2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_2_1a_math_v_advanced_linear_algebra_singular_value_06c82293_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For which matrix are the singular values exactly the absolute values of its eigenvalues?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&2\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}